/-!
# The first field comparison in DGS 8.6

Under the literal commuting-intersection condition of DGS 8.6(c), the
displacement of a moving center is bounded above by the opposite core index.
Across the complete local model catalogue, quadratic recognition bounds this
displacement below by the square of the moving quotient Sylow omega order,
unless that center has actual natural-module coordinates. Thus the two original
quotient omega orders satisfy the first comparison used in 8.6. If the terminal
quotient has two-rank one, the comparison forces naturality and identifies the
original commutator with the terminal center.

The reduced-source applications retain the earlier local-obstruction and model
inputs. Central containment remains explicit: the full converse in 8.6 still
requires the opposite comparison and the nonnaturality statement in 8.5.

Source: Delgado--Goldschmidt--Stellmacher, *Groups and Graphs* (1985),
Chapter 8, pp. 139--140, particularly the first inequality in the proof of 8.6.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section
